<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1712">1712</a>–<a href="/years/1786">1786</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Frederick II of Prussia · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick the Great, Frederick II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, was king from 1740. That year he invaded Silesia, citing old Hohenzollern claims to duchies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brzeg">Brzeg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krnov">Krnov</a>, and won most of it with the County of Kladsko in 1742. He occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a> when its princely line ended in 1744, held Silesia through the Seven Years' War and gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-prussia">West Prussia</a> in 1772. He was also a reforming ruler and patron of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great.jpg" alt="Frederick the Great" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Georg Ziesenis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_der_Gro%C3%9Fe_-_Johann_Georg_Ziesenis_-_Google_Cultural_Institute_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick was born on 24 January 1712 in the Berlin Palace, the son of Crown Prince Frederick William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. In his youth he was more interested in music and philosophy than war, which led to clashes with his authoritarian father. In 1730 he plotted to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> with Hans Hermann von Katte and other junior officers. The plan was betrayed and both were imprisoned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra">Küstrin</a> and accused of treason. The king had Katte beheaded on 6 November and forced his son to watch. On 12 June 1733 Frederick married Elisabeth Christine, a match arranged under Austrian influence; they had no children, and after his accession he separated from her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Frederick succeeded on 31 May 1740 to a highly militarised state: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s army was the fourth largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> though its population was only the twelfth largest, and the military absorbed 86 per cent of the budget. By 1770 he had doubled the army. After the death of Emperor Charles VI on 29 October 1740 he disputed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>'s right to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands and asserted a claim to Silesia, resting on old and ambiguous Hohenzollern claims. He withdrew from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a> once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> guaranteed his possession of Silesia, and used the peace after the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> to fortify the province and expand his army.</p><p>He wished to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> by annexing Polish territory, and had called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> an artichoke to be eaten leaf by leaf. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> gave him Royal Prussia in 1772, after which he styled himself King of Prussia. In 1778 he entered the War of the Bavarian Succession, in which he checked Austrian attempts to exchange the Austrian Netherlands for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. He commanded the army in sixteen major battles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In the Anti-Machiavel and his Political Testament Frederick wrote that the sovereign was the first servant of the state. With Grand Chancellor Samuel von Cocceji he reformed the courts, abolished most uses of judicial torture and removed special courts for special social classes, a reform completed after his death in the Prussian Law Code of 1794. He drained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oderbruch">Oderbruch</a> marshland and founded about 1,200 villages. A religious sceptic, he tolerated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Catholics, though not without prejudice. He played the flute, composed sonatas and met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> in 1747. He died on 17 August 1786, succeeded by his nephew Frederick William II.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Silesian Wars Saxony was defeated by Prussia at Kesselsdorf in 1745, and in 1756 <b>Frederick the Great</b> invaded without warning; the Saxon army capitulated at Pirna and was forced into Prussian service, and the country remained occupied for most of the Seven Years’ War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The monarchy then lost Silesia to <b>Frederick II of Prussia</b> in the War of the Austrian Succession, failed to recover it in the Seven Years’ War despite victory at Kolín in 1757, and suffered repeated defeats by revolutionary France after 1792.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Prussia’s first king was only ‘King in Prussia’, because part of Prussia still belonged to Poland; <b>Frederick II</b> took the title ‘King of Prussia’ after the partition of 1772.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1701 the army that the electors had built became the army of the Kingdom of Prussia, with which <b>Frederick the Great</b> conquered Silesia from 1740 and survived the Seven Years’ War of 1756–1763.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Frederick the Great</b>'s siege of Olomouc failed in 1758, Napoleon won the Battle of Austerlitz near Brno in 1805, and in 1866 Prussian troops occupied Brno before the preliminary peace of the Austro-Prussian War was signed at Mikulov (Nikolsburg).</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Frederick the Great</b> of Prussia corresponded with Voltaire and kept him at his court from 1750 to 1753; Catherine the Great of Russia based her Instruction of 1767 on Montesquieu and Beccaria; in Portugal the Marquis of Pombal rebuilt Lisbon after the earthquake on a regular plan and…</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1760" aria-label="Berlin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4850 352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Frederick the Great"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5300 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5300 622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,13.38&amp;year=1760">What was here in 1760? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-i-of-prussia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/frederick-william-i-of-prussia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Frederick William I of Prussia</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; 1688–1740</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…for in this Country every man must get to Heaven in his own way.&#8221;</p><footer>Marginal note on religious toleration, June 1740 &#183; tr. Thomas Carlyle, 1858-65 &#183; quoted in Thomas Carlyle, History of Friedrich II of Prussia &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2111/pg2111-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I intend, in spite of the Rules of Art, to attack Prince Karl's Army, which is nearly thrice our strength, wherever I find it.&#8221;</p><footer>Address to his generals at Parchwitz, December 1757 &#183; tr. Thomas Carlyle, 1858-65 &#183; as taken down at the meeting, quoted in Thomas Carlyle, History of Friedrich II of Prussia &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2118/pg2118-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Happy the moment when I took to training myself in philosophy! There is nothing else that can sustain the soul in a situation like mine.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to his sister Wilhelmine, July 1757 &#183; tr. Thomas Carlyle, 1858-65 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2118/pg2118-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am in the case of a traveller who sees himself surrounded and ready to be assassinated by a troop of cut-throats, who intend to share his spoils.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to his sister Wilhelmine, July 1757 &#183; tr. Thomas Carlyle, 1858-65 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2118/pg2118-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Happy, my dear Sister, is the obscure man, whose good sense from youth upwards, has renounced all sorts of glory; who, in his safe low place, has none to envy him, and whose fortune does not excite the cupidity of scoundrels!&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to his sister Wilhelmine, July 1757 &#183; tr. Thomas Carlyle, 1858-65 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2118/pg2118-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Gentlemen, I am undertaking a war in which I have no allies but your valor and your good will. My cause is just; my resources are what we ourselves can do; and the issue lies in fortune.&#8221;</p><footer>Address to his generals before invading Silesia, December 1740 &#183; tr. in The World's Famous Orations, ed. William Jennings Bryan, 1906 (translator not named) &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_7/To_His_Generals_Before_Invading_Silesia" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
